    <!-- HOW TO CHOOSE -->
    <section class="content-section" id="how-to-choose">
      <h2>How to Choose the Right Loan Calculator</h2>
      <p>Not all loans work the same way. Before you plug in numbers, make sure you're using the right tool for your situation.</p>

      <h3>SBA Loans vs. Conventional Business Loans</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>SBA loans</strong> (7(a) and 504) are partially guaranteed by the federal government,
        which lets lenders offer lower rates and longer terms than a conventional bank loan — but the
        application is more involved and approval can take weeks. Use the
        <a href="/loan-calculators/sba-loan-calculator">SBA Loan Calculator</a> if you're applying
        through an SBA-approved lender and need to model exactly what the government-backed repayment
        looks like.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Conventional business loans</strong> are faster to obtain but typically carry higher
        rates and shorter terms. Use our <a href="/loan-calculators/business-loan-calculator">Business Loan Calculator</a>
        to compare both scenarios side-by-side so you can see which saves you more over the full repayment window.
      </p>

      <h3>Secured vs. Unsecured Loans — Why the Rate Differs</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Secured loans</strong> (SBA, land, auto, RV) are backed by collateral — the lender
        can claim the asset if you default. Because the lender's risk is lower, rates are lower.
        <strong>Unsecured loans</strong> (personal loans, some business lines of credit) carry no
        collateral, so interest rates run higher — typically 10%–36% depending on credit. Always run
        both scenarios through the appropriate calculator before committing, to determine total cost
        of borrowing, not just monthly payment size.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- QUICK CHECKLIST -->
    <section class="content-section" id="loan-checklist">
      <h2>What to Look for in Any Loan</h2>
      <p>Monthly payment is only one number. Before signing, confirm these five factors with your lender:</p>
      <ol class="hub-checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>APR vs. interest rate.</strong>
          The interest rate is the base cost of borrowing. The <em>APR (Annual Percentage Rate)</em>
          includes fees like origination charges, SBA guarantee fees, and closing costs — it's the true
          annual cost. Always compare APRs across lenders, not just rates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Origination and guarantee fees.</strong>
          SBA 7(a) loans carry a guarantee fee of 0.5%–3.5% of the guaranteed portion, depending on loan
          size and term. Conventional lenders may charge 1%–5% origination. These are often financed into
          the loan — meaning you pay interest on them for the full term.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Prepayment penalties.</strong>
          SBA 7(a) loans with terms &ge;15 years have a sliding prepayment fee in years 1–3 (5% / 3% / 1%).
          Many conventional lenders charge a flat prepayment fee. If there's any chance you'll refinance
          or pay off early, factor this in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Variable vs. fixed rate.</strong>
          Variable rates (common with SBA 7(a)) can drop if Prime falls — but also rise. Fixed rates give
          payment certainty for the full term. Model both with the calculator above using your lender's
          quoted ceiling rate to stress-test your budget.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Total cost of borrowing.</strong>
          A longer term lowers your monthly payment but dramatically increases total interest paid. Use
          the calculator's "Total Amount Paid" figure — not just the monthly payment — when comparing
          loan offers.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

